Savane offers a few Git hooks configurable at
/git/admin/?group=<unix_group_name>; they use the pre-receive
and the post-receive hook.  The earliest Git release supported is 1.7.9
where GnuPG signatures were introduced.

The built-in pre-receive hooks are,

* Checking GnuPG signatures.  When enabled, Git rejects any pushes
  containing commits lacking valid signatures made by a key from
  the configured set.

* Forbidding merges.  When enabled, Git rejects any pushes
  containing merge commits.

* Closing, opening and commenting on tracker items.  When enabled, the hook
  looks for trailers in the commit messages formatted like,

    <Tag>: <tracker> #<item_id> <comment>

  Where <Tag> is 'Closes-Item', 'Opens-Item' or 'Updates-Item', respectively
  (the tags are case-insensitive); <tracker> is 'bugs', 'task', 'support'
  or 'patch'; <item_id> is the number of the item in question; <comment>
  is a comment string, it's mandatory for the 'Updates-Item' tag and optional
  when closing and opening items.  On attempts to modify items in other groups
  or non-existing items, the push is rejected.

The only built-in post-receive hook sends notifications to the configured
comma-separated list of email addresses.

Additional hooks external to Savane can be installed
in the hooks/savane.d/pre-receive.d and hooks/savane.d/post-receive.d
directories.  Each hook should be an executable complying with the respective
Git hook interface.  The pre-receive hooks are run before Savane internal hooks,
and the push is rejected when the first script exits with a non-zero code;
likewise, the post-receive hooks are run before Savane internal hooks,
but their exit codes are ignored.

The Web UI at /git/admin/?group=<unix_group_name> allows enabling and disabling
all hooks, both built-in and external, and selecting the Git branches to apply
each hook to.

